Parquet writer: column-scoped compression codec and level.

GlamDB lets a collection choose the Parquet compression codec and level for
its data files, with per-column overrides. The pinned ParquetWriter applies
only write.parquet.compression-codec and write.parquet.compression-level to
every column. This patch adds the column-scoped forms, named like Iceberg's
other column-scoped properties (write.metadata.metrics.column.<name>):

  write.parquet.compression-codec.column.<field-name>
  write.parquet.compression-level.column.<field-name>

The suffix is the exact name of a top-level primitive field. A column codec
without a column level uses that codec's own default level: levels are codec
specific and are never inherited from the file-level setting. A column level
without a column codec, an unknown column, an unavailable codec, a level for a
codec without levels and a level outside the codec's range are rejected in
Open(), before the output file is created. The file-level level is range
checked the same way (Arrow does not validate levels; an invalid one would
otherwise fail only while pages are compressed).

Pinned revision: apache/iceberg-cpp 64dc66f159bd62c9817a96d74cbce767268934d0
(v0.4.0); upstream main 36e0d1a has no column-scoped Parquet properties.

